Nevertheless, recent empirical studies have yielded mixed and sometimes conflicting evidence and there remains a lack of consensus on the effect of agriculture on economic growth. While some researchers contend that agricultural development is a precondition to industrialization and economic growth, others strongly disagree and argue for a different path. Several authors assert that growth in the overall economy depends on the development of the agricultural sector (Schultz 1964; Gollin et al 2002). Anecdotal evidence and basic economic data from recent decades suggest that agriculture plays a pivotal role in the national economy of developing countries. Relative to the United States, gross domestic product (GDP) per capita in Sub-Saharan Africa and Latin America over the past three decades has been very low. In Sub-Saharan Africa, GDP per capita grew at 2.1% from 1990 to 2010 while Latin America observed only a 1.6% growth rate for the same time period. In contrast, Asia’s GDP per capita had a remarkable 6.9% growth rate per year during the same period.1 Several Asian economies (e.g., China and Thailand) have experienced phenomenon growth. Interestingly, much of the countries with poor growth records still have a relatively large share of their GDP coming from agriculture. For most of the African countries, agriculture accounts for over 20% of GDP. The debate on the developmental role of agriculture is ongoing. This current analysis attempts to bridge the gap in the empirical literature on the dynamic interaction between agriculture and economic growth. The objective of this study is to reexamine the causal relationship between agriculture and economic growth by applying recent advances in econometric analysis and newly developed algorithms of inductive causation (directed acyclic graphs [DAGs]) to national data from a diverse group of nine developing and transition economies in Sub-Saharan Africa, Asia, and Latin America. Specifically, this study investigates the causal relationship between agriculture and economic growth at three different time horizons: contemporaneous time, short-run, and long-run. First we use the error correction modeling approach to investigate both short-run dynamics and long-run causal relationships between agriculture and economic growth. Then, DAG technique is used to investigate contemporaneous causal relationships and to provide overidentifying restrictions on the innovations from a vector error correction model (VECM). In contrast to prior analyses, the application of DAG techniques allows for the examination of both contemporaneous and dynamic causal structure of the linkages between agriculture and economic growth. To our knowledge, this paper is the first study that explores the contemporaneous information flow between agriculture and GDP growth in developing economies. The empirical results suggest that the causal direction of the relationship between agriculture and economic growth varies by country. For several of the countries examined, the results from the empirical analysis provide support for the agriculture-led growth (ALG) hypothesis such that investment in agriculture could serve as an engine of economic growth in contemporaneous time. In contrast, we also found evidence in support of a long-run causal flow from economic growth to agriculture implying that growth in the aggregate economy could serve as a catalyst for growth in the agricultural sector. This finding suggests that a vibrant aggregate economy and a productive agricultural sector can coexist in developing countries in their pursuit of sustainable development. The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. The second section contains a brief
