sey’s RESET test), heteroskedasticity (White’s test), and structural stability (CUSUM and CUSUMSQ tests).
DAGs and Innovation Accounting
When the evidence from the trace and lambda max cointegration test statistics suggests that the variables in the system are cointegrated, it is appropriate to estimate the models with error correction terms which could be used to capture long-run relationships. However, the individual coefficients from the ECM are difficult to interpret. Innovation accounting (via impulse response function [IRF] and forecast error variance decomposition [FEVD]) was used to analyze the dynamic relationship among the variables in the system. As in previous studies, we identified the contemporaneous causal structure on innovations from the VECM with the aid of causal paths suggested by DAG analysis (Swanson and Granger 1997; Bessler and Akleman 1998; Awokuse 2005; Awokuse et al 2009). The majority of past investigations of causal relationships among economic variables use the Granger (1969) causality framework that builds on the knowledge that a cause
