EMPIRICAL RESULTS
The data set used in the analysis consists of annual observations for a selection of nine3 developing and transition economies from three regions: Asia (China, Indonesia, and Thailand); Latin America (Brazil, Chile, and Mexico); and Sub-Saharan Africa (Cameroon, Kenya, and South Africa). We specifically focused on developing countries because of the controversial role of agriculture in the ongoing debate on the “best” path to economic development for low-income countries. China, Brazil, and South Africa are the leading economies in Asia, Latin America, and Sub-Saharan Africa, respectively. Indonesia and Thailand were chosen because their agricultural productivity performance is representative of Asian region. Although their economies greatly depend on agriculture, they are making steady progress in developing industrial sectors. Chile and Mexico are major agricultural exporters in Latin America. Their agricultural sectors seem to play an important role in their overall economy. In Cameroon and Kenya, agriculture is a major contributor to GDP. Cameroon represents some countries with great endowment, like petroleum, cocoa, and coffee. Kenya represents some countries where the economies heavily depend on rain-fed agriculture and tourism, thus are vulnerable to fluctuations in the business cycles. Due to data availability, the empirical analyses were conducted using data from 1980 to 2011 for the nine countries. Although the key relationship of interest is that between real GDP growth and agriculture, three additional control (exogenous) variables were included in the estimated models. The variables used in the analysis are as follows: net real GDP (real GDP minus agricultural value added; Y), agricultural value added (A), gross capital formation as proxy for capital (K), real exports (X), and TOT (T). All data series are in natural logarithms and were obtained from the World Bank’s World Development Indicators (http://data.worldbank.org/data-catalog/world-development-indicators).
