Contemporaneous Causality: DAG Results
While multivariate cointegration analysis provides information on long-run relationships, it lacks information on causal direction in both contemporaneous and dynamic (lagged) time. Hence, we present results from DAGs, which provide richer and useful information about the complex contemporaneous interrelationships between the variables in a system. Figure 1 contains DAG results based on innovations from the cointegrated VAR model. Among other causal linkages presented in the DAGs, of most interest is the linkage between the agricultural sector and aggregate economic growth. Specifically, in Latin America, the evidence from DAG indicates that causality flows from GDP growth to agriculture in Chile and Mexico, but there is insignificant evidence of causal linkages in Brazil. The contribution of agriculture to economic growth in contemporaneous time appears to be stronger in the Asian region where causality flows from agriculture to GDP growth in all three countries (China, Indonesia, and Thailand). This result is consistent with conclusions from several previous studies, which contends that
