FEVDs and Impulse Responses Results
Although DAG is an invaluable tool for sorting out the nature of the causal linkages between variables in a system, it focuses on statistical significance and lacks information on economic significance or the strength of these causal relationships. Some contemporaneous causal flows in Figure 1 can be fragile due to their economic insignificance. Hence, better inference that captures short-run dynamic linkages can be made with innovation accounting which uses a combination of DAG and the DAG-based IRFs and FEVDs. Swanson and Granger (1997) showed that contemporaneous causal flows suggested by DAG analysis are useful for addressing issues of identification of VAR residuals used in computing IRFs. Bessler and Akleman (1998) extended Swanson and Granger (1997) by applying DAG to innovations from a VAR model. This current study adopts the approach first proposed in Bessler and Akleman (1998). Using the estimates from an ECM specification of Equation (4), the causal analysis is extended by examining both FEVDs and IRFs. FEVDs are contributions of each source of innovations to the variance of the n-period ahead forecast error for each endogenous variable, at various horizons. The hypothesis of GLA is supported if innovations in agriculture are the major source of the non-self variations in GDP growth. Alternatively, the ALG hypothesis is supported if the converse is observed. The variance decompositions are obtained for horizons of 1, 2, 3, and 10
