SUMMARY AND CONCLUDING REMARKS
This paper reexamines the nature of the causal relationship between agriculture and economic growth in a selection of nine developing countries in Sub-Saharan Africa, Asia, and Latin America. In recent decades, the potential contribution of agriculture to economic growth has been a source of debate on divergent viewpoints. While some contend that agricultural development is a precondition to industrialization, others strongly disagree and argue for a different path. Despite much debate and qualitative analyses of the contribution of agriculture to economic growth and development, few empirical investigations on this issue exist. This paper uses recent methods of inductive causation to reexamine the role of agriculture as an “engine of growth.” The study analyzed data for nine developing and transition economies in Sub-Saharan Africa, Asia, and Latin America with the aid of the time-series techniques and DAGs. Results from the empirical analysis provide evidence indicating that the direction of causal relationship between agriculture and GDP growth depends on the country and could either support the GLA or ALG hypotheses. In contemporaneous time, some of the countries show that agriculture stimulates aggregate economic growth. In addition, the empirical evidence also indicates that the reverse causality also exists for some countries such that a vibrant economy could be a catalyst for expansion in the agricultural sector. The results also show that a long-run relationship exists between economic growth and agriculture. Also, empirical results from IRFs and variance decompositions also support a bidirectional causal link between agriculture and GDP growth. Furthermore, the results suggest that trade openness, TOT, and capital accumulation also make contribution to GDP growth.
