As a form of sensitivity test, we obtained data for three developed countries (the United States, the United Kingdom, and Germany) and conducted complementary analyses for each country as with the nine developing countries. For each developed country, the cointegration results show that a long-run relationship exists between GDP growth and agriculture. However, the results are mixed with regard to the direction of causality between agriculture and GDP growth. The test results from innovation accounting (FEVD and IRF) show support for the GLA hypothesis for Germany and the United Kingdom. In contrast, empirical evidence for the United States does not show a strong causal linkage between agriculture and GDP growth.7 In summary, the empirical evidence from our analyses is mixed and varies across countries. In some cases, there is strong evidence in support of the ALG hypothesis. In contrast, the results for some countries suggest that having a vibrant aggregate economy is a prerequisite for agricultural development. Overall, due to the mixed nature of current empirical results, the findings from this study should not be generalized to all developing countries. In a small way, our results suggest that caution should be taken before massive investment is made in agriculture, before first understanding the complex interactions of various sectors of the economy. Nevertheless, in cases where agriculture is shown to stimulate economic growth, more investment in the agricultural sector is desirable. This is particularly needed in many developing countries where agriculture has been marginalized. In some cases, the demise of the agricultural sector has been driven by domestic policies that intentionally promoted industrialization-led development while marginalizing the agricultural sector. Future research on this issue could expand the methodological approach advocated in this study to a larger cross-section of countries including developed economies.
